Top ML Papers of the Week  
Welcome to the repository where we explore the top machine learning papers of the week. This week's highlights (Feb 16 - Feb 23) feature groundbreaking advancements in areas like causal reasoning in LLMs, retrieval-augmented generation, scalable inference, and knowledge-enhanced reasoning.

 🧠Top Papers

Do Large Language Models Reason Causally Like Us? Even Better?  
This paper explores whether LLMs reason causally like humans or even surpass human capabilities. It systematically evaluates various LLMs on causal inference tasks, comparing their performance against human benchmarks. The study provides insights into how well these models understand cause-and-effect relationships and whether they can generalize to novel causal scenarios.  
📄 [Read more](https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.10215)

A Survey on LLM-powered Agents for Recommender Systems  
With the rise of LLMs in personalization, this paper surveys their applications in recommender systems. It categorizes different LLM-powered recommendation strategies, including content-based filtering, collaborative filtering, and hybrid approaches. The paper also discusses the challenges in using LLMs for recommendations, such as fairness, scalability, and interpretability.  
📄 [Read more](https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.10050)

LaRA: Benchmarking Retrieval-Augmented Generation and Long-Context LLMs - No Silver Bullet for LC or RAG Routing  
Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) is a popular paradigm for improving LLM outputs by leveraging external knowledge. This paper presents LaRA, a benchmark evaluating various RAG and long-context (LC) models. It highlights key challenges, including retrieval latency, hallucination risks, and the trade-offs between different retrieval strategies.  
📄 [Read more](https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.09977)

λScale: Enabling Fast Scaling for Serverless Large Language Model Inference  
This paper introduces λScale, a new framework for dynamically scaling LLM inference in serverless environments. λScale optimizes resource allocation and reduces cold-start latency, making serverless deployment more efficient for real-time applications. The results show significant improvements in cost efficiency and performance compared to traditional auto-scaling techniques.  
📄 [Read more](https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.09922)

KnowPath: Knowledge-enhanced Reasoning via LLM-generated Inference Paths over Knowledge Graphs  
LLMs often struggle with multi-step reasoning over structured knowledge. KnowPath proposes a method that integrates LLM-generated inference paths with knowledge graphs to improve reasoning accuracy. The approach enhances factual consistency and reduces hallucinations, making LLMs more reliable for complex reasoning tasks.  
📄 [Read more](https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.12029)

ReVISE: Learning to Refine at Test-Time via Intrinsic Self-Verification  
ReVISE introduces a novel self-verification mechanism for LLMs, allowing them to refine their own outputs at test time. The method leverages intrinsic uncertainty estimation to detect potential errors and iteratively improve predictions. Experimental results demonstrate significant accuracy gains across various reasoning and NLP benchmarks.  
📄 [Read more](https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.14565v1)

WavRAG: Audio-Integrated Retrieval Augmented Generation for Spoken Dialogue Models  
Extending RAG to multimodal tasks, WavRAG incorporates audio retrieval into language model generation. It improves spoken dialogue systems by grounding responses in retrieved speech data, reducing hallucinations in voice assistants and conversational AI. The study evaluates WavRAG across multiple datasets, showcasing its superior performance over traditional text-only RAG models.  
📄 [Read more](https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.14727)

Plan-over-Graph: Towards Parallelable LLM Agent Scheduling  
Efficiently scheduling tasks for LLM-powered agents remains a challenge. Plan-over-Graph introduces a new planning approach that enables parallel execution of subtasks in agent workflows. The method enhances response time, efficiency, and scalability, making LLM agents more practical for real-world applications.  
📄 [Read more](https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.14563v1)

